This calculation accounts for binomial uncertainty and the quoted field-frequency uncertainty, but it does not appear to include several relevant sources of uncertainty:

- planet-radius uncertainties and uncertainty in the radius-valley location;
- uncertainty in stellar metallicities and the binary [Fe/H]>0 classification;
- sample-selection effects;
- uncertainty in detection completeness;
- possible differences in metallicity and stellar-mass distributions within the metal-rich bin;
- uncertainty associated with the choice of field comparison sample.

The main categories are:

1. systems with only sub-Neptunes;
2. systems with only super-Earths;
3. mixed systems.

This is a reasonable classification, but it differs from asking whether the presence of any sub-Neptune or any super-Earth correlates with cold Jupiters. Mixed systems contain both types and are not included in the “sub-Neptune” or “super-Earth” conditional frequencies. This distinction matters.
